THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL

No third-party LIBRARY is vendored here. The practice targets under `foreign-agent/` and
`foreign-langchain/` are wrappers this project wrote around libraries installed separately —
smolagents (Apache 2.0, Hugging Face) and LangChain (MIT) — and `external/nemo/` is a
configuration for NVIDIA NeMo Guardrails (Apache 2.0). The libraries themselves are not
included; each is installed from its own distribution under its own licence.

Two target configs are the exception, and it is a deliberate one.
`redteam/targets_foreign.yaml` and `redteam/targets_foreign_code.yaml` reproduce the SYSTEM
PROMPTS that smolagents ships with its `ToolCallingAgent` and `CodeAgent`, verbatim, under
Apache 2.0 and with attribution to Hugging Face. They are there because this engine refuses to
read a target's prompt out of its own source: a detector that has seen the prompt can subtract
it, and a control whose prompt was read rather than declared is not a control. Declaring them
is what makes the measurement honest, and reproducing them is what declaring them costs.

`dvla/` holds an untracked third-party practice application with its own licence, NOT
tracked in this repository. `redteam/targets_dvla.py` will tell you so if you try to use it
without cloning it first.

The attack corpus is original. Several techniques it exercises are described in published
research and named in the comments beside the attacks that use them — ArtPrompt, Context
Compliance Attacks, Crescendo, Skeleton Key, policy puppetry, latent injection. The techniques
are cited; no text was taken from any other tool's corpus, and every attack here is aimed at a
canary this project plants rather than at a secret described in the abstract.
